Done. `rename` is implemented and verified in the disposable run root.

What changed (all under `/Users/tijs/projects/local-model-bench/runner/runs/tmpfyad5s9x/run/rust/`):

- `src/store.rs:39` — new `pub fn rename(dir: &Path, old_id: &str, new_id: &str, new_title: &str) -> std::io::Result<()>`:
  - Reads `{old_id}.note` (`NotFound` if absent), parses it with the same `splitn(4, "\n---\n")` as `load_all`, and validates that the in-file id equals `old_id` (else `InvalidData`) — so a stray/malformed file is rejected before anything is mutated.
  - Rewrites the file as `{new_id}.note`, copying the pinned flag and body fields byte-for-byte from the parsed parts (title replaced with `new_title`). Tags need no handling: `load_all` derives them from the body at read time, so they follow the body automatically.
  - Deletes the old `{old_id}.note` when the ids differ; if a note already existed under `new_id`, it is replaced (consistent with `save`'s overwrite semantics). No trace of the old id remains.
- `tests/rename.rs` — new integration test file, 5 tests: full rename preserving body/tags/pinned + exactly one note in `load_all` + no old-id file left; rename-to-same-id (single file, title updated); collision with an existing `new_id` note; `NotFound` for missing file; `InvalidData` for in-file id mismatch.

Verification: `cargo test` → all 7 tests pass (2 pre-existing + 5 new), `cargo clippy --all-targets` clean. Workspace isolation respected — only the disposable run root was touched; no commit made.

One design note: the write-then-delete order means a crash mid-rename leaves a duplicated note rather than a lost one. Say the word if you'd like the stricter atomic variant (rename file, then rewrite content in place).


